      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(whatsapp): use canonical media primitives",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhatsApp maintained local copies of outbound media loading, audio transcoding, MIME classification, and base64 validation even though canonical media owners already existed. The copies had drifted: injected media readers could silently widen local-file access, image optimization was not forwarded through the shared loader, and malformed base64 uploads could reach permissive decoding.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis moves WhatsApp onto the existing canonical media paths and deletes its local outbound-media loader.\n\n- Outbound loading now uses `plugin-sdk/outbound-media`; the shared facade only gains forwarding for its already-supported `optimizeImages` option.\n- Voice transcoding now uses the existing `transcodeAudioBufferToOpus`; the helper only gains typed output-container and maximum-duration options so WhatsApp can preserve Ogg/Opus output and its duration cap.\n- MIME normalization and classification now use the existing `plugin-sdk/media-mime` helpers.\n- Upload buffers now use the existing SDK base64 estimator and strict canonicalizer before decoding.\n\nWhatsApp-specific behavior stays local: MP3, M4A, and WebM inputs still transcode; only Ogg/Opus is accepted directly as a voice note; document naming remains transport-specific; and the legacy retry helper is intentionally untouched.\n\nThe final diff is deliberately narrow: 12 files, 184 additions, and 185 deletions. It touches only WhatsApp and the two existing shared owners that needed small typed/forwarding additions.\n\n## User Impact\n\nSupported WhatsApp media sending and voice-note behavior remain unchanged. Injected media readers must provide an explicit local-root boundary. Malformed WhatsApp upload buffers are rejected by strict base64 canonicalization, and configured size limits are enforced from the encoded-size estimate before allocating the decoded buffer.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 113 focused tests passed across WhatsApp, audio transcoding, and the outbound-media SDK facade.\n- Core and extension TypeScript checks passed.\n- `pnpm plugin-sdk:surface:check` passed without adding an SDK entry point.\n- The full build passed.\n- Blacksmith Testbox run: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29662927176\n- Fresh autoreview against the actual PR base reported no accepted or actionable findings.\n",
